[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Inheritance and Code Reuse in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance is one of the fundamental pillars of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP). It allows software developers to create new classes based on existing classes, promoting code reuse, logical organization, and seamless maintenance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"At its core, inheritance models an "is-a" relationship. For instance, a 'SportsCar' is a specialized type of 'Car'. Instead of rewriting the engine mechanics and wheel configurations for every vehicle type, the 'SportsCar' class can inherit these attributes and behaviors from a base 'Car' class."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Base Class (Parent/Superclass): The general class whose properties and methods are inherited."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Derived Class (Child/Subclass): The specialized class that inherits from the base class and can add or override functionality."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Implementation Example"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let us explore a practical example using Python to demonstrate how inheritance reduces code duplication."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def make_sound(self):
        return "Some generic sound"

class Dog(Animal):
    def make_sound(self):
        return "Bark""},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the 'Dog' class inherits the '__init__' constructor from the 'Animal' class, while overriding the 'make_sound' method to provide specific behavior."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Code Reuse"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Redundancy: Common logic is written once in the superclass."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintainability: Updating the base class automatically propagates changes to derived classes."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extensibility: New features can be added to subclasses without modifying existing, tested code."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering inheritance, developers can build scalable, modular architectures. However, it is essential to design hierarchies carefully to avoid overly complex dependency trees, often favoring composition over deep inheritance chains when appropriate."}]
